          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How is COPD different from asthma?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nBased on the reference material I could retrieve, these are the main differences. The retrieved passages do not give a full side-by-side comparison, so this is only part of the picture.\n\n**Asthma** (from the asthma guideline, \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\"): Symptoms are variable wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough. They are often worse at night or early in the morning, and they can be set off by exercise, allergens, cold air or viral infections. Diagnosis needs both a matching symptom pattern and breathing tests showing that airflow changes over time and improves with a bronchodilator (a medicine that opens the airways). A normal test on a good day does not rule it out.\n\n**COPD** (from the general reference \"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)\"): It is a long-term condition that usually gets worse slowly over years. Typical features are breathlessness on activity, chronic cough and sputum (phlegm) production, usually in someone with a relevant exposure history. Tobacco smoking is the main cause in high-income countries. Other causes include smoke from indoor cooking or heating, occupational dust and fumes, and air pollution. Diagnosis requires spirometry (a breathing test). It is not made on symptoms alone.\n\n**Both can occur together.** The coding references (\"Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes\" and \"Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes\") note that clinical records can document asthma and COPD at the same time, and the coding rules require both to be recorded rather than choosing one.\n\n**Not covered by these passages:** how the two conditions are treated, and how they differ in outlook. If you want to know more, a clinician or pharmacist can explain how these apply to a particular person.",
